MOOR now has a Chief-facing control plane anchored by `context/chief` and `apps/launcher-cli/src/chief.ts`.

Reusable behaviors include:

- visible prioritized work ledger with agent handoffs;
- app/capability/plugin catalog;
- deterministic reuse-vs-extend-vs-compose-vs-build-new resolution;
- static dead/incomplete-control candidate audit;
- plugin inventory;
- current City local launch;
- Chief status/snapshot output.

The Chief ledger is product-facing queue/status state. Repository Anchors remain the authoritative autonomous-work history.

The Chief ledger has an installable offline-first phone client at `apps/web/public/colony/` (served from the static web deploy at `/colony/`). It is a dependency-free static app (HTML + service worker + manifest), not a framework surface.

Reusable behaviors include:

- token-based GitHub contents-API read/write of `context/chief/TASKS.json` and read of `APPS.json` — the repo itself is the sync backend, no server needed;
- op-log sync: local edits apply instantly, queue as ops, and replay onto the freshest server copy on reconnect (remote edits are rebased, never clobbered);
- plain-language sync states (Up to date / Waiting to sync / Offline — saved on this phone / Sync failed);
- reuse of the ledger's own `statusModel` for all status changes — no invented states.

Anchor: `docs/anchors/history/2026-09-20-anchor-0039-chief-phone-app.md`.

MOOR already has the pieces for a hosting-independent application lifecycle:

- `MoorPack` package contract with per-file SHA-256 integrity;
- creator draft/release publishing;
- public/free app package retrieval;
- entitlement-backed package retrieval;
- local managed installation;
- atomic update/rollback behavior;
- AppRunner execution of packaged browser apps;
- launcher publishing through `moor publish` / `moor chief publish`.

Normal app updates should use this path rather than being copied into the web host and forcing a site rebuild.

MOOR already has:

- public product/tool routes;
- discovery/search;
- product detail surfaces;
- free public runnable browser tools;
- Saved state;
- Owned/Entitlement-backed access;
- creator/public product analytics;
- shareable public product links.

Relevant web areas include `MoorSurface`, `ToolsSurface`, `AssetRouteSurface`, consumer API clients, tool routes, and analytics helpers.

MOOR collects its own traffic, signup, order, and ad-click numbers instead of relying only on third-party scripts.

- public beacon `POST /v1/analytics/events` writes validated events to `analytics_events` (`0228_analytics_events.sql`); the web client fires it from `trackPageView`/`trackEvent` on production hosts only;
- admin-only aggregates under `/v1/admin/analytics/` (overview KPIs with prior-period comparison, per-day trend series, top pages, monetization breakdown), guarded by `requireUser` plus the admin-role check from `admin.ts`;
- public contracts live in `@moor/contracts` (`analytics.ts`); the admin dashboard is the deep-linked `analytics` surface in `AnalyticsSurface.tsx`, written in plain founder-friendly language.

Do not expose raw event streams or per-visitor detail in the UI; the dashboard shows aggregates only.

MOOR Newsroom is the native source-linked current-information capability.

- public, crawlable routes: `/news`, `/news/:slug`, `/topic/:slug`, and `/feeds/:slug`;
- owner-scoped custom feeds with private/public visibility, source selection, refresh history, and schedules;
- bounded allowlisted source adapters, deduplication, an original metadata-based editorial brief, source attribution, and outbound-source links;
- immutable, structured MOOR-authored article revisions with evidence records, review-before-publish validation, explicit publish/rollback/withdraw controls, and editor-only API access;
- Saved integration and aggregate story view/source-click measurement;
- sitemap entries, canonical URLs, OpenGraph metadata, JSON-LD, and an edge-rendered crawl fallback;
- a Netlify scheduled function that processes a small due-feed batch every 30 minutes.

The durable data owner is PostgreSQL (`0209_newsroom.sql`); public projection always checks that a story belongs to at least one public feed. Private feeds must never become public through route, sitemap, analytics, or SEO behavior.

The editorial rule is strict: MOOR may publish its own concise, source-linked briefing and direct readers to the original source, but must not reproduce or disguise another publisher’s article body. A source brief remains the public fallback until an authorized editor explicitly publishes a reviewed MOOR article revision; prior revisions can be restored and withdrawal returns the story to the source brief. The current first slice covers AI; expand source/category coverage only after the production launch loop is verified.

This capability is implemented on `feat/moor-newsroom` but is not production-proven until its Netlify database, deployment, scheduler, public routes, sitemap/canonicals, and real analytics are verified.

MOOR already supports a connected creator flow with:

- creator Assets/drafts;
- publishing/public releases;
- USD pricing;
- public sales URLs;
- server-created paid checkout;
- verified paid-order confirmation;
- Entitlement granting after confirmed payment;
- creator views/runs/saves/revenue analytics.

Do not build a parallel checkout or ownership model for a new product type unless the existing commerce boundary cannot represent it coherently.

The current MOOR City test runtime includes a reusable deterministic landmark compiler in `products/moor-city/standalone-city-v515.js`.

Its stable test boundary is `window.MOORMegaSpawn`:

- `describe()` returns the generated global scene blueprint, level contract, circulation contract, chunk ports, bounds and performance policy;
- `regenerate(form)` rebuilds the landmark from an explicit form;
- `randomize()` creates and builds a new deterministic seed;
- `getForm()` and `stats()` expose the current generator state without requiring scene traversal;
- `setDebug(boolean)` shows or hides global chunk seams;
- `reset()` returns to the canonical Horizon Ark form.

The compact authoring form is owned by device-local storage at `moor.horizon-ark.lab.v1`. Generated Three.js objects are transient runtime state and are rebuilt from the form and seed.

The compiler solves one global coordinate frame, floor elevations, circulation, walkable surfaces, chunk boundaries and ports before allowing chunk-local variation. Repeated detail uses shared materials and instancing; far structure, mid detail and near detail are separated so landmark scale does not require full-detail rendering at every distance.

This implementation remains a test-world capability. Consumers may reuse the scene-description and chunk contract, but should not treat Horizon Ark as the permanent Skyblock spawn until the real Three.js scene has passed rendered inspection, traversal and performance verification.

The web host exposes a capability layer through `window.MOOR.capabilities` and iframe messaging.

Current capability patterns include:

- capability discovery/listing;
- direct invocation;
- natural-message routing;
- account/device capability memory where needed.

This is the preferred direction for cross-app behaviors that should be callable by future chats/tools without duplicating state.

A persistent `floor-dca` capability exists as an example of a reusable decision engine with:

- deterministic recommendation logic;
- device/account memory;
- decision history;
- outcome/lesson storage;
- shared invocation through the capability bridge.

Its existence is architectural precedent, not a requirement that unrelated features use the same persistence model.

Reusable checks already exist for:

- architecture;
- launch readiness;
- MOOR City product rules;
- lint;
- typecheck;
- tests;
- API bundling;
- full builds.

Use these instead of inventing overlapping validation scripts unless a new invariant cannot be represented by an existing check.

Promote a feature into shared capability infrastructure when at least one is true:

- multiple products/surfaces need the same behavior;
- the behavior owns persistent cross-surface state;
- the behavior is valuable as a stable primitive for future creators/apps;
- duplicating it would create inconsistent business/domain rules.

Do not promote one-off product UI merely because abstraction is possible.

Whenever a reusable capability changes, record:

- what it does;
- its stable invocation boundary;
- its state owner;
- important constraints;
- where consumers should integrate with it.

Keep this file brief enough that an agent can scan it before implementing a new idea.
